---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
const pageUrl = (path) => `${base}${path}`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Local LLM Benchmark Lab — Anirudh Bompada"
  description="Independent llama.cpp benchmark testing: repeatable model configurations, throughput measurements, and data collection."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Personal project / Local AI</p>
    <h1>Benchmarking models on <span class="accent">real hardware.</span></h1>
    <p class="page-intro">
      I use llama.cpp and repeatable prompts to compare how local GGUF models behave
      under different quantizations, context lengths, and GPU offloading settings.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-grid">
    <aside>
      <p>MODELS / GGUF</p>
      <p>RUNNER / LLAMA.CPP</p>
      <p>RESULTS / CSV</p>
      <p>STATUS / ONGOING</p>
    </aside>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What the test records</h2>
      <p>
        Each run keeps the model and quantization, context length, GPU-layer setting,
        token limit, prompt, raw llama.cpp output, and measured prompt and generation
        throughput together. Results go into structured CSV files so I can compare
        coding and general-purpose prompts without losing the exact command used.
      </p>

      <h2>One reproducible configuration</h2>
      <p>
        A reference test uses Qwen2.5-Coder-3B-Instruct at Q4_K_M with a 4,096-token
        context, 99 requested GPU layers, and a 128-token generation limit. Keeping
        those settings alongside each prompt makes later comparisons meaningful.
      </p>
      <p>
        Prompt length, output length, GPU availability, and context settings all affect
        throughput. I am keeping individual performance results offline until I have
        reviewed which comparisons are useful to share.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the project is going</h2>
      <p>
        I am extending the measurements to compare additional model sizes and contexts,
        monitor GPU and system resources, and summarize multiple runs rather than treating
        one fast result as a conclusion.
      </p>
      <a class="text-link" href={pageUrl('projects')}>Back to all projects</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
